    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I guess I'm just finding it hard to see how the \"living tree\" idea can be anything but subjective. If there's no fixed standard, aren't we basically saying the Constitution doesn't actually exist as a rule of law, but is just a collection of whatever values the current majority happens to have? It feels like originalism is the only way to actually prevent judicial activism. Wouldn't you agree that originalism is more philosophically sound because it maintains the separation of powers?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You have made a very strong point regarding the potential subjectivity of the \"living tree\" doctrine, as critics accurately argue it can blur the line between interpretation and policy making.\n\nWhile originalism is often championed for its fidelity to the **separation of powers** by limiting judicial overreach, living constitutionalists counter that strict originalism may actually hinder democratic accommodation of evolving societal needs.\n\nThe tension you identified is the central divide in American jurisprudence, where the legitimacy of any approach depends on whether one values historical certainty more than the democratic capacity to change the law to fit modern values.",
